{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed218530468/1/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ff2825db\" lines=\"18-36\">\n<description>\nSelect the majority interior marker color, then redraw the framed board as that color's frame plus its bounding-box outline.\n\nInput structure:\n- The top row is a copied palette made from adjacent equal color pairs.\n- Below it is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame around a black interior.\n- Non-black, non-frame interior pixels are marker colors; one marker color has\n  the unique highest count.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the palette row unchanged.\n- Pick the uniquely most frequent interior marker color.\n- Clear the lower board to black, recolor its outer frame with the picked color,\n  and draw the picked color's interior bounding-box border.\n\nKey insight:\n- The noisy marker cloud is not preserved as pixels; only the majority marker\n  color and its spatial extent define the output.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9720b24f\" lines=\"34-46\">\n<description>\nDelete each foreign-color object whose bounding box is contained by a larger object of another color.\n\nInput structure\n- The black pixels are the empty canvas, and every other color forms one or more 8-connected monochromatic objects.\n- Some larger foreground objects act as loose U/C-like containers around smaller objects of different colors.\nRule\n- Find all 8-connected monochromatic non-black objects.\n- Recolor an object to black exactly when its bounding box is contained in the bounding box of a larger object of another color.\n- Keep all other pixels unchanged.\nKey insight\n- The removable objects are identified by containment in another color's rectangular extent, not by their own color or shape.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1a244afd\" lines=\"49-68\">\n<description>\nRotate each magenta source pixel 90 degrees counterclockwise around its matched blue pivot, recoloring the new pixel orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The only foreground colors are blue pivot pixels and magenta source pixels.\n- Each magenta source can be paired one-to-one with a blue pivot in the same\n  row or column.\n\nRule:\n- Remove all magenta source pixels.\n- For each matched source/pivot pair, preserve the source distance from the\n  pivot but rotate its offset 90 degrees counterclockwise.\n- Paint the rotated in-bounds target orange; if the target is outside the\n  grid, the source simply disappears.\n\nKey insight:\nThe blue pixels are rotation centers, and row/column alignment gives a\nunique global matching from each magenta source to its pivot.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"305b1341\" lines=\"22-37\">\n<description>\nExpand each sparse color object into a filled rectangular region using its legend-paired fill color.\n\nInput structure\nA background-color grid has a two-column legend in columns 0 and 1, starting at row 0: each row gives\na source color in the left column and that source color's fill color in the right column.  Outside the\nlegend, the foreground pixels are sparse dot objects whose colors are exactly the legend source colors.\n\nRule\nClear the legend.  For each source color, take the bbox of all of its non-legend dots, expand that bbox\nby one pixel in every direction, and fill background cells inside the expanded rectangle with the paired\nlegend fill color while preserving every original non-legend dot.\n\nKey insight\nThe legend order is also the layer order: earlier legend rows are in front, so fill rectangles are painted\nfrom the last legend row back to the first and higher-priority fills overwrite lower-priority fills.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e3fe1151\" lines=\"36-55\">\n<description>\nFill the one blank in each corner region so all four regions have the same color multiset.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant background color forms one solid separator row and one solid\n  separator column.\n- The separator cross splits the grid into four equal rectangular corner regions.\n- Each corner region contains exactly one background-colored blank pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Count the non-background colors in each corner region.\n- The completed region multiset is defined by the maximum observed count of\n  each color across the four regions.\n- Replace each region's blank pixel with the single color needed to match that\n  completed multiset.\n\nKey insight:\n- The separator color inside a corner region is a missing value marker; comparing\n  the four regions reveals the shared multiset without using numeric color order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"221dfab4\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nPropagate a repeating striped pattern from a yellow border segment toward the opposite edge, recoloring foreground objects on certain stripes.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Foreground blobs of another color scattered in the interior.\n- A short yellow (4) segment sits on one border edge.\n\nRule:\n- From the yellow segment, march toward the opposite edge in steps of 2 cells, cycling colors Y, Y, G (4, 4, 3). The 1-cell gap between stripes is forced to bg.\n- Yellow stripes paint only within the segment's span.\n- Green stripes additionally recolor all non-bg pixels across the entire row/column to green.\n\nKey insight: Green stripes act as a \"flood\" that recolors existing objects on the full row/column, while yellow stripes are confined to the segment span.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"df978a02\" lines=\"57-72\">\n<description>\nLet the largest center-pointing object grow outward while every other object loses its inward tip.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a single dominant background color and four 8-connected monochromatic foreground objects.\n- The objects surround a central background gap and point toward it from cardinal directions.\n- Exactly one object has the largest area.\n\nRule\n- Find the central background gap by minimizing aggregate distance to the four objects.\n- Recolor the nearest-to-center pixel of each non-largest object to background.\n- Add a three-pixel cap in the largest object's color on the side away from the center, centered on the shared row or column.\n\nKey insight\n- The edit is a transfer of prominence around a common center: losers retract at their tips, and the unique largest object extends outward from the center.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"53fb4810\" lines=\"105-119\">\n<description>\nExtend marker tiles outward from '+' shapes by repeating them to the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A dominant non-bg color forms several '+'-shaped objects.\n- Small colored patches (1–few cells) sit adjacent to each arm of a '+'.\n\nRule:\n- For each marker tile touching a '+', determine which side of the '+' it sits on.\n- Project the tile away from the '+' in that direction, repeating the tile pattern periodically until the grid boundary.\n- '+' pixels are never overwritten.\n\nKey insight: Each marker's attachment side determines its extension direction; the pattern repeats periodically, not as a single copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"985ae207\" lines=\"82-97\">\n<description>\nRepeat each 3x3 control-center sprite toward the matching solid rectangle until a copy first overlaps that rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each sprite is a 3x3 foreground object whose eight border pixels have one color and whose center has a different control color.\n- For every control color, there is exactly one separate solid rectangular object of that color.\n\nRule\n- Match each sprite to the unique solid rectangle whose color equals the sprite center.\n- Copy the whole 3x3 sprite in the cardinal direction of that rectangle, stepping by three cells each time.\n- Stop after pasting the first copy whose 3x3 bbox overlaps the rectangle; copied pixels overwrite the rectangle there.\n\nKey insight\nThe center pixel is not the paint color: it is a destination key, while the entire 3x3 sprite is the motif tiled along the straight path.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"538b439f\" lines=\"122-142\">\n<description>\nReflect the solid rectangular source objects across the full separator line and color the gap with the separator color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background contains one full-width or full-height one-pixel\n  separator line in a non-background color.\n- One other foreground color forms solid rectangular objects, all strictly on the same side of that separator.\n- Remaining non-background pixels are scattered distractors.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input grid shape and unchanged pixels outside each affected corridor.\n- Mirror each source object across the separator line.\n- For every source object, recolor the rectangular strip between the\n  original object and its reflection to the separator color, then paint the\n  reflected object in the source color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The full straight line is a mirror axis; the real objects are identified\n  structurally as the only solid rectangles confined to one side, while\n  scattered pixels are noise that may be overwritten inside the corridor.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e048c9ed\" lines=\"11-26\">\n<description>\nAdd a quantity-color label in the top marker column for each horizontal bar.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a black background and one non-background marker pixel in the top row.\n- Below the marker row, every non-empty source row contains one solid monochrome horizontal bar to the left of the marker column.\n- Source rows are staggered every other row.\n\nRule\n- Copy the input grid.\n- For each source bar, read its quantity as the bar length.\n- Paint the quantity color in the marker column on the same row as the bar:\n  2->1, 3->4, 4->9, 5->6.\n\nKey insight\n- The grey marker supplies the result column, while each bar supplies a small quantity with a fixed color code.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6ffbe589\" lines=\"83-98\">\n<description>\nCrop a board cluster and rotate its color layers based on outside cue sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A main multi-color board cluster (largest foreground region).\n- Small monochrome cue sprites outside the board for the colors that rotate.\n\nRule:\n- Tight-crop around the board cluster.\n- For each color layer inside the crop, find its same-color cue outside.\n- Rotate that layer clockwise by (cue pixel count mod 4) × 90°.\n- No outside cue → no rotation.\n\nKey insight: Cue sprite size mod 4 encodes rotation amount for its matching color layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"332202d5\" lines=\"49-64\">\n<description>\nFill every row from the nearest marked horizontal bar, turning bar rows and color-boundary ties into separator rows.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background fills the grid except for full-width horizontal bars and one vertical marker column.\n- Outside the marker column, every row is monochrome: either background or a source bar color.\n- Background rows share one marker color in the marker column; source bar rows share the other.\n\nRule\n- Each non-source row takes the color of the nearest source bar, with that row's marker-column pixel set to the source-marker color.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport itertools, numpy as np\n\ndef connected(mask):\n    pts=list(zip(*np.where(mask)))\n    if not pts: return False\n    seen={pts[0]}; stack=[pts[0]]\n    while stack:\n        r,c=stack.pop()\n        for dr,dc in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)):\n            nr,nc=r+dr,c+dc\n            if 0<=nr<mask.shape[0] and 0<=nc<mask.shape[1] and mask[nr,nc] and (nr,nc) not in seen:\n                seen.add((nr,nc)); stack.append((nr,nc))\n    return len(seen)==len(pts)\n\ndef trim(mask):\n    rows=np.where(mask.any(axis=1))[0]\n    cols=np.where(mask.any(axis=0))[0]\n    return mask[rows[0]:rows[-1]+1, cols[0]:cols[-1]+1]\n\ndef compress(mask):\n    out=mask.copy()\n    keep=[0]\n    for i in range(1,out.shape[0]):\n        if not np.array_equal(out[i], out[i-1]):\n            keep.append(i)\n    out=out[keep]\n    keep=[0]\n    for j in range(1,out.shape[1]):\n        if not np.array_equal(out[:,j], out[:,j-1]):\n            keep.append(j)\n    out=out[:,keep]\n    return out\n\ndef canon(mask):\n    opts=[]\n    for m in (mask, np.flipud(mask), np.fliplr(mask), np.flipud(np.fliplr(mask))):\n        opts.append((m.shape, m.tobytes()))\n    return min(opts)\n\ncand=[]\nfor h in range(2,4):\n    for w in range(2,4):\n        for bits in range(1,1<<(h*w)):\n            mask=np.array([(bits>>k)&1 for k in range(h*w)], dtype=bool).reshape(h,w)\n            if not connected(mask):\n                continue\n            if mask.sum()<3:\n                continue\n            t=trim(mask)\n            if t.shape\"'!=(h,w):\n                continue\n            good=True\n            for i in range(h-1):\n                if np.array_equal(t[i], t[i+1]): good=False\n            for j in range(w-1):\n                if np.array_equal(t[:,j], t[:,j+1]): good=False\n            if not good:\n                continue\n            cand.append(t)\nkeys={canon(c):c for c in cand}\nprint('\"'cands', len(cand), 'unique', len(keys))\nfor i,m in enumerate(keys.values()):\n    print(i, m.shape, int(m.sum()))\n    print(m.astype(int))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport itertools, numpy as np\n\ndef connected(mask):\n    pts=list(zip(*np.where(mask)))\n    if not pts: return False\n    seen={pts[0]}; stack=[pts[0]]\n    while stack:\n        r,c=stack.pop()\n        for dr,dc in ((1,0),(-1,0),(0,1),(0,-1)):\n            nr,nc=r+dr,c+dc\n            if 0<=nr<mask.shape[0] and 0<=nc<mask.shape[1] and mask[nr,nc] and (nr,nc) not in seen:\n                seen.add((nr,nc)); stack.append((nr,nc))\n    return len(seen)==len(pts)\n\ndef trim(mask):\n    rows=np.where(mask.any(axis=1))[0]\n    cols=np.where(mask.any(axis=0))[0]\n    return mask[rows[0]:rows[-1]+1, cols[0]:cols[-1]+1]\n\ndef compress(mask):\n    out=mask.copy()\n    keep=[0]\n    for i in range(1,out.shape[0]):\n        if not np.array_equal(out[i], out[i-1]):\n            keep.append(i)\n    out=out[keep]\n    keep=[0]\n    for j in range(1,out.shape[1]):\n        if not np.array_equal(out[:,j], out[:,j-1]):\n            keep.append(j)\n    out=out[:,keep]\n    return out\n\ndef canon(mask):\n    opts=[]\n    for m in (mask, np.flipud(mask), np.fliplr(mask), np.flipud(np.fliplr(mask))):\n        opts.append((m.shape, m.tobytes()))\n    return min(opts)\n\ncand=[]\nfor h in range(2,4):\n    for w in range(2,4):\n        for bits in range(1,1<<(h*w)):\n            mask=np.array([(bits>>k)&1 for k in range(h*w)], dtype=bool).reshape(h,w)\n            if not connected(mask):\n                continue\n            if mask.sum()<3:\n                continue\n            t=trim(mask)\n            if t.shape\"'!=(h,w):\n                continue\n            good=True\n            for i in range(h-1):\n                if np.array_equal(t[i], t[i+1]): good=False\n            for j in range(w-1):\n                if np.array_equal(t[:,j], t[:,j+1]): good=False\n            if not good:\n                continue\n            cand.append(t)\nkeys={canon(c):c for c in cand}\nprint('\"'cands', len(cand), 'unique', len(keys))\nfor i,m in enumerate(keys.values()):\n    print(i, m.shape, int(m.sum()))\n    print(m.astype(int))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"cands 86 unique 30\n0 (2, 2) 3\n[[0 1]\n [1 1]]\n1 (2, 3) 4\n[[0 1 0]\n [1 1 1]]\n2 (2, 3) 4\n[[1 1 0]\n [0 1 1]]\n3 (2, 3) 5\n[[1 0 1]\n [1 1 1]]\n4 (3, 2) 4\n[[0 1]\n [1 1]\n [0 1]]\n5 (3, 2) 4\n[[1 0]\n [1 1]\n [0 1]]\n6 (3, 2) 5\n[[1 1]\n [0 1]\n [1 1]]\n7 (3, 3) 5\n[[1 0 0]\n [1 1 0]\n [0 1 1]]\n8 (3, 3) 6\n[[0 0 1]\n [0 1 1]\n [1 1 1]]\n9 (3, 3) 6\n[[0 0 1]\n [1 0 1]\n [1 1 1]]\n10 (3, 3) 5\n[[0 1 0]\n [1 1 1]\n [0 0 1]]\n11 (3, 3) 6\n[[0 0 1]\n [1 1 1]\n [0 1 1]]\n12 (3, 3) 5\n[[1 0 0]\n [1 1 1]\n [0 0 1]]\n13 (3, 3) 6\n[[0 0 1]\n [1 1 1]\n [1 0 1]]\n14 (3, 3) 5\n[[0 1 0]\n [1 1 0]\n [0 1 1]]\n15 (3, 3) 6\n[[0 1 0]\n [0 1 1]\n [1 1 1]]\n16 (3, 3) 5\n[[0 1 0]\n [1 1 1]\n [0 1 0]]\n17 (3, 3) 6\n[[0 1 0]\n [1 1 1]\n [0 1 1]]\n18 (3, 3) 6\n[[0 1 0]\n [1 1 1]\n [1 0 1]]\n19 (3, 3) 6\n[[0 1 1]\n [0 0 1]\n [1 1 1]]\n20 (3, 3) 5\n[[1 1 0]\n [0 1 0]\n [0 1 1]]\n21 (3, 3) 6\n[[0 1 1]\n [0 1 0]\n [1 1 1]]\n22 (3, 3) 7\n[[0 1 1]\n [1 0 1]\n [1 1 1]]\n23 (3, 3) 6\n[[0 1 1]\n [1 1 0]\n [0 1 1]]\n24 (3, 3) 7\n[[1 1 0]\n [0 1 1]\n [1 1 1]]\n25 (3, 3) 7\n[[1 0 1]\n [1 1 1]\n [0 1 1]]\n26 (3, 3) 7\n[[1 1 0]\n [1 1 1]\n [0 1 1]]\n27 (3, 3) 7\n[[1 0 1]\n [1 1 1]\n [1 0 1]]\n28 (3, 3) 7\n[[1 1 1]\n [0 1 0]\n [1 1 1]]\n29 (3, 3) 8\n[[1 1 1]\n [1 0 1]\n [1 1 1]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
